    bio: "Antoine-Laurent de Lavoisier was born in Paris in 1743 into a prosperous legal family, and from an early age displayed an exceptional aptitude for rigorous, quantitative thinking. He studied law to please his father but spent every spare hour pursuing natural philosophy, geology, and the emerging science of chemistry. His early work on lighting systems for the streets of Paris won him a gold medal from the Académie des Sciences at the age of twenty-two, and he was elected a full member of the Academy by twenty-five — an almost unprecedented distinction.\n\nLavoisier\'s central achievement was nothing less than the overthrow of phlogiston theory, the dominant chemical paradigm of his age. Chemists of the time believed that combustion and rust were caused by the release of a mysterious substance called phlogiston. Through a series of meticulous experiments with precise balances — he was among the first scientists to insist on careful quantitative measurement as the basis of chemical argument — Lavoisier demonstrated that combustion was not a loss of phlogiston but a combination with oxygen, a gas he named and whose role he defined. He also showed that water was a compound of hydrogen and oxygen rather than a primordial element, and he co-authored the first systematic chemical nomenclature, the basis of the naming system still used today.\n\nBeyond pure chemistry, Lavoisier served as an administrator of the Ferme Générale, the private consortium that collected taxes on behalf of the French Crown — a position that made him wealthy and, fatally, marked him as an enemy of the Revolution. He worked throughout the 1780s on practical problems of public benefit: improving gunpowder production, reforming the prison system, advising on hospital ventilation, and investigating the mesmerism craze of Franz Anton Mesmer. His laboratory on the Boulevard de la Madeleine, run in close partnership with his brilliant wife Marie-Anne, who translated English scientific papers and illustrated his great textbooks, was the most productive chemical workshop in Europe.\n\nWhen the Revolution turned radical, the tax-farming connection proved deadly. In 1794, Lavoisier was arrested along with twenty-seven other former tax farmers, tried before a Revolutionary tribunal, and guillotined on the 8th of May — the same day the verdict was delivered. He was fifty years old. The mathematician Joseph-Louis Lagrange remarked the following day that it had taken only a moment to cut off that head, and a hundred years might not produce another like it. Within two years, the Revolutionary government acknowledged that the trial had been a travesty, rehabilitated his name, and returned his confiscated papers to his widow. His *Traité Élémentaire de Chimie*, published in 1789, stands as the founding document of modern chemistry."
